var definitions = ['<h2>Tú dormías cuando llegué.</h2><div><strong>Tú</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dormías</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "dormir", which means "to sleep". It describes a continuous or habitual past action that was happening at the moment of another action.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction meaning "when". It introduces a dependent clause describing the time at which the main action took place.</div><div><strong>llegué</strong> This is the first-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "llegar", which means "to arrive". It describes a completed action in the past, the moment the speaker arrived.</div>','<h2>Te ves bonita cuando sonríes.</h2><div><strong>Te</strong> This is the second-person singular reflexive pronoun in Spanish, used here as part of the reflexive construction "Te ves" (you look) that indicates the action refers back to the subject, which is "you".</div><div><strong>ves</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "ver", which means "to see" in Spanish. In the reflexive form "te ves", it is used to mean "you look" or "you appear".</div><div><strong>bonita</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "pretty" or "beautiful". It’s in the feminine singular form to agree with the subject being described, which is implied to be a female person due to the gender of the adjective.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that translates to "when" in English. It’s used to introduce the dependent clause "cuando sonríes".</div><div><strong>sonríes</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "sonreír", which means "to smile" in Spanish. It indicates the action being performed by the subject "you" in the time when she appears "pretty" or "beautiful".</div>','<h2>Llámame cuando llegues a casa.</h2><div><strong>Llámame</strong> This is the imperative form of the verb "llamar" in Spanish, which means "to call". Here, it’s used in the affirmative command form for the second-person singular, essentially saying "call me".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that translates to "when" in English. It introduces a subordinate clause indicating the time at which the action should take place.</div><div><strong>llegues</strong> This is the present subjunctive form of the verb "llegar", which means "to arrive". The subjunctive mood is used because the action is not certain; it’s hypothetical or dependent on a condition (in this case, the arrival at home).</div><div><strong>a casa</strong> The preposition "a" translates to "to" in English here, and "casa" means "home". Together, "a casa" indicates the destination where the arrival is expected.</div>','<h2>Los niños se divierten cuando están en el patio de recreo.</h2><div><strong>Los</strong> This is the definite article for plural masculine nouns in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>niños</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "children". It is in the plural form.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun which is part of a reflexive verb. In this sentence, it’s used with "divierten" to express that the children are entertaining themselves or having fun.</div><div><strong>divierten</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the reflexive verb "divertirse", meaning "to have fun" or "to enjoy oneself".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "when".</div><div><strong>están</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be". It describes a state of being, location, or a temporary condition.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish meaning "in" or "on".</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the definite article for singular masculine nouns in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>patio</strong> This is a noun in Spanish meaning "yard" or "playground".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is another preposition in Spanish, which is often translated as "of" or "from". It denotes possession or relation.</div><div><strong>recreo</strong> This is a noun meaning "recreation" or "recess". When combined with "patio", it refers to "playground" or "recess yard".</div>','<h2>Ellos van a la piscina cuando hace calor.</h2><div><strong>Ellos</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>van</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "ir", which means "to go". It indicates the action that the subject is undertaking.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated as "to" in English. It is used here to indicate direction.</div><div><strong>la piscina</strong> The definite article "la" means "the" in English, and "piscina" translates to "pool". This phrase "la piscina" means "the pool".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This word means "when" in English. It introduces a time clause that specifies the conditions under which the action takes place.</div><div><strong>hace</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "hacer", which means "to make" or "to do". In this context, it translates to "it is" when talking about the weather.</div><div><strong>calor</strong> This noun translates to "heat" or "warmth" in English. In this sentence, "hace calor" means "it’s hot" or "it’s warm".</div>','<h2>Creo que el tigre está enojado.</h2><div><strong>Creo</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "creer", which means "to think" or "to believe". It represents the speaker’s belief or opinion.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction used in Spanish, equivalent to "that" in English. It is used here to introduce a dependent clause.</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used to specify or refer to a particular noun that is masculine and singular.</div><div><strong>tigre</strong> This is a noun in Spanish, meaning "tiger". It refers to the large cat species known for their striped coats.</div><div><strong>está</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be". It is used to describe a temporary state or condition.</div><div><strong>enojado</strong> This is an adjective in Spanish, meaning "angry" or "upset". In this sentence, it matches the masculine singular form to agree with "tigre". If the tiger were known to be female, it would be "enojada".</div>','<h2>¡Espero que tengas hambre!</h2><div><strong>¡Espero</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "esperar", which means "to hope" or "to expect" in English. It expresses the speaker’s hope or expectation.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word translates to "that" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a dependent clause.</div><div><strong>tengas</strong> This is the second-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. The subjunctive mood is used because it follows a verb expressing a wish, hope, or expectation (espero).</div><div><strong>hambre!</strong> This is a noun meaning "hunger" in English. Here, it is used to express the state of being hungry. The exclamation mark at the end conveys emotion or emphasis.</div>','<h2>Es importante que comprendas.</h2><div><strong>Es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser" in Spanish, which means "to be". It denotes a state of being, indicating that something is important in this case.</div><div><strong>importante</strong> This is an adjective in Spanish that means "important". It agrees with the subject in gender and number, although "it" as an implied subject is not explicitly expressed in Spanish.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish, which in this sentence introduces a dependent clause. It can often be translated as "that" in English.</div><div><strong>comprendas</strong> This is the second-person singular present subjunctive tense form of the verb "comprender" in Spanish, which means "to understand". The subjunctive mood is used here to express a wish, desire, or hope.</div>','<h2>Me dijeron que estabas en casa.</h2><div><strong>Me</strong> This is the first-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "me" in English. It indicates that the speaker is the recipient of the action.</div><div><strong>dijeron</strong> This is the third-person plural preterite tense form of the verb "decir", which means "to say" or "to tell". It refers to a completed action in the past, where "they" is implied as the subject of the action.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction used to introduce a dependent clause, equivalent to "that" in English.</div><div><strong>estabas</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be". The imperfect tense is used for ongoing or repeated actions in the past, here implying you were at home for some duration of time.</div><div><strong>en casa</strong> This is a Spanish phrase meaning "at home". "En" translates to "in" or "at", and "casa" means "home" or "house".</div>','<h2>Sé que me mentiste.</h2><div><strong>Sé</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "saber", which means "to know". It indicates that the speaker is aware or certain of the information that follows.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction used to connect clauses, commonly translated as "that" in English. It introduces a dependent clause in the sentence.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a first-person singular reflexive pronoun in Spanish. It’s used as an indirect object pronoun here, meaning "to me" or "me" in English.</div><div><strong>mentiste</strong> This is the second-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "mentir", which means "to lie". It indicates that the action of lying was performed in the past, and the person lied to was the speaker, as indicated by the indirect object pronoun "me" preceding the verb.</div>','<h2>¡Qué estudiante!</h2><div><strong>¡Qué</strong> This is an exclamatory word in Spanish used to express emotion or surprise. It translates to "What a" in English when used in this context, to modify and emphasize the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>estudiante</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "student". It can refer to anyone who is studying in an educational setting, such as a school, college, or university. The word is gender-neutral by itself, but can be made specifically feminine by adding an "a" to become "estudiantea" in a non-standard manner, as the standard feminine form would simply be "estudiante" as well.</div>','<h2>¡Qué artista tan importante!</h2><div><strong>¡Qué</strong> This is an exclamatory word used to express admiration or surprise in Spanish. It’s similar to the English word "what" when used in phrases like "What a beautiful day!" It leads the exclamatory phrase.</div><div><strong>artista</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "artist". It could refer to someone involved in any form of the arts - visual, performing, literary, etc. It is in singular form and is a common gender noun.</div><div><strong>tan</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish equivalent to the English "so" or "such" when used before an adjective. It serves to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>importante</strong> This is a Spanish adjective that means "important". In the context of this sentence, it’s used to qualify the noun "artista", indicating that the artist in question is of great significance or prominence.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Tocaba la guitarra cuando era joven.</h2><div><strong>Tocaba</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "tocar" in Spanish, which means "to play" an instrument. This suggests a repeated or habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>la guitarra</strong> This is a Spanish noun phrase meaning "the guitar". "La" is the definite article "the", used here for a feminine singular noun. "Guitarra" is the Spanish word for "guitar".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "when". It’s used to introduce a time clause.</div><div><strong>era</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "ser" in Spanish. "Ser" means "to be" and the imperfect tense indicates a continuous or habitual past action, in this case, describing a past state of being.</div><div><strong>joven</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "young". Here, it describes the subject of the sentence in the past.</div>','<h2>Le llamaremos cuando su orden esté lista.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is a third-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish. It’s used here to indicate the person to whom the action of the verb will be directed, equivalent to "to him", "to her", or "to you" (formal).</div><div><strong>llamaremos</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense form of the verb "llamar", which means "to call". It indicates that the action of calling will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "when". It introduces a dependent clause that specifies the time in which the action of the main clause will occur.</div><div><strong>su</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "your" in the formal sense, or "his" or "her". It indicates ownership or relation to the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>orden</strong> This is a noun that means "order" in the context of a request, instruction, or purchase.</div><div><strong>esté</strong> This is the third-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "estar" which means "to be". The subjunctive mood is used here because it follows a conjunction that expresses a future condition or event that is uncertain or pending.</div><div><strong>lista</strong> This is an adjective meaning "ready" or "prepared". In this context, it describes the state of the "orden" (order).</div>','<h2>Ella sonrió cuando le mostré la foto.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is a Spanish third-person singular personal pronoun, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sonrió</strong> This is a form of the Spanish verb "sonreír", which means "to smile". It’s in the preterite tense, third-person singular, indicating past completed action. Here, it tells what the subject did.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This Spanish conjunction means "when". It introduces a time-dependent clause, indicating the moment something happened.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in Spanish, which corresponds to "him", "her", or "you (formal)" in English. It indicates to whom the action of the verb is directed.</div><div><strong>mostré</strong> This is the first-person singular form of the verb "mostrar", which means "to show", in the preterite tense. It indicates that the speaker is the one who performed the action of showing, and it happened in the past.</div><div><strong>la foto</strong> This phrase means "the photo" in English. "La" is the feminine singular definite article, and "foto" is a feminine noun meaning "photo" or "picture". It’s the direct object of the sentence, receiving the action of the verb "mostrar".</div>','<h2>Cuando intento frenar, no responden bien.</h2><div><strong>Cuando</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction meaning "when". It is used to introduce a time-dependent clause.</div><div><strong>intento</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "intentar", which means "to try" or "to attempt". It indicates the action that the speaker is undertaking.</div><div><strong>frenar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the Spanish verb meaning "to brake" or "to stop". It specifies the action that the speaker attempts to carry out.</div><div><strong>no</strong> This is an adverb that negates or denies something. In this sentence, it is used to indicate that the action of braking is not being accomplished successfully.</div><div><strong>responden</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "responder", which means "to respond" or "to react". It refers to how the brakes (implied) are performing in response to the attempt to brake.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is an adverb meaning "well" in English. In this sentence, it indicates the manner in which the brakes are responding, or in this case, are not responding properly when the attempt to brake is made.</div>','<h2>Ella aprende mejor cuando estudia fuera.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aprende</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aprender", which means "to learn". It indicates an action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>mejor</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish meaning "better". It describes the manner in which the subject learns.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish meaning "when". It introduces the time clause that specifies the conditions under which the subject learns better.</div><div><strong>estudia</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estudiar", which means "to study". It’s part of the time clause and further describes the action the subject is taking under specific conditions.</div><div><strong>fuera</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish meaning "outside". In this sentence, it specifies the location where the studying (and therefore the learning) takes place.</div>','<h2>Ellos piden ayuda cuando no comprenden.</h2><div><strong>Ellos</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of men or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>piden</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "pedir", which means "to ask for" or "to request". In this sentence, it indicates that the action (asking for help) is done by the subject (they).</div><div><strong>ayuda</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "help" or "assistance". In this context, it is the direct object of the verb "piden", indicating what they are asking for.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction meaning "when". It introduces a subordinate clause that indicates the circumstances under which the action of the main clause (asking for help) takes place.</div><div><strong>no</strong> This is an adverb of negation in Spanish. It negates the verb that follows it, in this case, "comprenden".</div><div><strong>comprenden</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "comprender", which means "to understand". With the negation "no" before it, the phrase "no comprenden" means "they do not understand". It’s the verb of the subordinate clause introduced by "cuando".</div>','<h2>Ella se despertará cuando su alarma suene.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "She" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun. It indicates that the subject of the verb performs an action on herself. In this sentence, it forms part of the reflexive future tense construction.</div><div><strong>despertará</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "despertar", which means "to wake up". The future tense indicates that the action will occur at a later time.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish equivalent to "when" in English. It introduces a dependent clause that specifies the circumstances under which the main action will take place.</div><div><strong>su</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "her" in English. It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>alarma</strong> This is a noun that means "alarm" in English. It’s the object that the possessive adjective "su" is modifying.</div><div><strong>suene</strong> This is the third-person singular present subjunctive tense of the verb "sonar", which means "to sound". The subjunctive mood is used after certain expressions and conjunctions to express doubt, wishes, or hypothetical situations. Here, it is used with "cuando" to indicate a future event that has not yet occurred.</div>','<h2>Cuando seas grande, vas a manejar un auto.</h2><div><strong>Cuando</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction meaning "when". It is used to introduce a clause indicating the time at which the action of the verb will take place.</div><div><strong>seas</strong> This is the second-person singular future subjunctive form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It expresses a future condition or event regarded as potential or possible. This form is used because it is depending on the condition of the person being "grande" (big or adult).</div><div><strong>grande</strong> This is a Spanish adjective that means "big" or "adult". When referring to age, it can be translated as "grown-up".</div><div><strong>vas</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "ir", which means "to go". In this context, it’s part of the periphrasis "vas a" to form the near future tense, indicating what the person is going to do.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a Spanish preposition which is part of the periphrasis "vas a" used to form the near future tense when combined with an infinitive verb. In English, it’s similar to the word "to" in this structure.</div><div><strong>manejar</strong> This is the Spanish infinitive of the verb meaning "to drive". In this sentence, it is used to express what action will be taken in the near future.</div><div><strong>un auto</strong> This is a noun phrase meaning "a car". "Un" is the masculine singular indefinite article equivalent to "a" in English, and "auto" is a colloquial term for "automóvil", which means "car".</div>','<h2>Nosotras manejábamos cuando el motor se detuvo.</h2><div><strong>Nosotras</strong> This is the first-person plural, feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "we" in English. It specifies that the subjects are all female.</div><div><strong>manejábamos</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "manejar", which means "to drive". It indicates that the action was ongoing or repeated in the past.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is the conjunction "when" in English. It’s used to introduce a time clause that relates to the moment when another action occurred.</div><div><strong>el motor</strong> This phrase translates to "the motor" or "the engine" in English. "El" is the masculine singular definite article, and "motor" is a masculine singular noun, referring to the engine of a vehicle.</div><div><strong>se detuvo</strong> This phrase consists of the reflexive pronoun "se" and the third-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "detener", meaning "stopped" or "ceased". The reflexive form implies that the action happened to the engine itself.</div>','<h2>Tú estás siempre contenta cuando te despiertas.</h2><div><strong>Tú</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English when talking in a familiar context. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>estás</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be" in English when referring to temporary states or locations. Here, it indicates the current state of the subject.</div><div><strong>siempre</strong> This word means "always" in English. It’s an adverb describing the frequency of how often the subject is in the state of being content.</div><div><strong>contenta</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "happy" or "content". It’s in the feminine singular form to agree with the subject if the speaker were female. For a male subject, it would be "contento".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This word translates to "when" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a subordinate clause indicating the time at which the subject is content.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is the second-person singular reflexive pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "yourself" in English. It’s used with reflexive verbs, and here it indicates that the action of waking up is performed by the subject upon themselves.</div><div><strong>despiertas</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the reflexive verb "despertarse", which means "to wake up". It describes the action of the subject waking up.</div>','<h2>El piso se pone resbaladizo cuando está mojado.</h2><div><strong>El</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish for masculine, singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>piso</strong> This is a masculine, singular noun in Spanish meaning "floor" or "ground".</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in Spanish. It is part of a reflexive construction ("se pone") and indicates that the action of the verb is performed on the subject itself, in this case, the floor.</div><div><strong>pone</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "poner", which means "to put" or "to become". In this reflexive construction, it means "becomes".</div><div><strong>resbaladizo</strong> This is an adjective in Spanish meaning "slippery". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, "piso".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "when". It is used to introduce a dependent clause that specifies the time at which the action takes place.</div><div><strong>está</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be". It describes a temporary state or condition.</div><div><strong>mojado</strong> This is a past participle used as an adjective in Spanish meaning "wet". It agrees in gender and number with the noun "piso" and it indicates the condition of the floor that causes it to become slippery.</div>','<h2>Yo vivía en una granja cuando era más joven.</h2><div><strong>Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vivía</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "vivir", which means "to live". It describes a past habitual action or a past state of being.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated to "in" in English. It’s used here to indicate the location where the subject lived.</div><div><strong>una granja</strong> This phrase means "a farm" in English. "Una" is a feminine singular indefinite article meaning "a", and "granja" is a feminine singular noun meaning "farm".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "when". It introduces a time clause and relates to the time when the subject lived on the farm.</div><div><strong>era</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It’s used here to describe a past state of being, in this case, the subject’s younger age.</div><div><strong>más joven</strong> This phrase means "younger" in English. "Más" is an adverb meaning "more", and "joven" is an adjective meaning "young". Together, they describe the subject’s age at the time.</div>','<h2>Usted se reirá cuando deje de ser tan serio.</h2><div><strong>Usted</strong> This is the formal second-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se reirá</strong> This is a future tense conjugation of the reflexive verb "reírse", which means "to laugh". "Se" is the reflexive pronoun for "usted" and "reirá" is the third-person singular future tense of "reír", so together it means "you will laugh".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction that translates to "when" in English. It introduces a dependent clause that describes the condition under which the action of the main clause will take place.</div><div><strong>deje</strong> This is the present subjunctive form of the verb "dejar", which means "to stop" or "to leave". It’s used after "cuando" to express a condition or action in the future that is not certain to occur.</div><div><strong>de ser</strong> This is the infinitive "ser", meaning "to be", preceded by the preposition "de", which together can mean "from being" or "to stop being" depending on the context.</div><div><strong>tan serio</strong> The word "tan" translates to "so" in English and is an adverb of degree, modifying the adjective "serio", which means "serious". Together, "tan serio" means "so serious". It describes the extent of the seriousness that the subject would need to stop in order for the action "you will laugh" to happen.</div>','<h2>Sí, llegué al aeropuerto cuando el avión despegó.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It confirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>llegué</strong> This is the first-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "llegar", which means "to arrive". The speaker is indicating that they arrived at a specific point in the past.</div><div><strong>al</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "a" (meaning "to") and the definite article "el" (meaning "the"). In English, this combination is not contracted and is expressed as "to the".</div><div><strong>aeropuerto</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "airport". It is a masculine singular noun and the place where the speaker arrived.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish meaning "when". It is used to introduce a time clause, indicating the moment something happened.</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish meaning "the". It is used here to specify the subject—"the airplane"—as a particular one that is known to both the speaker and the listener/reader.</div><div><strong>avión</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "airplane". It is masculine singular and is the subject of the subordinate clause in the sentence.</div><div><strong>despegó</strong> This is the third-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "despegar", which means "to take off". This describes an action that the airplane completed in the past, specifically at the moment the speaker arrived at the airport.</div>','<h2>Ella se irá cuando haya terminado todo su trabajo.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the Spanish third-person singular feminine pronoun, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se irá</strong> This is a conjugation of the reflexive verb "irse", which means "to leave" or "to go away", in the third-person singular future tense. In this context, it means "she will leave" or "she will go".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction that means "when" in English. It introduces a subordinate clause indicating the time at which the action of the main verb will take place.</div><div><strong>haya terminado</strong> This is the present perfect subjunctive form of the verb "terminar", which means "to finish". The subjunctive mood is used because it refers to an action that has not yet been completed and is dependent on the condition specified in the main clause.</div><div><strong>todo</strong> This is an adjective that means "all" or "every" in English. Here, it modifies "su trabajo", indicating the entirety of the work.</div><div><strong>su</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "her" or "his" in English. It indicates that the work belongs to the subject of the sentence, "ella".</div><div><strong>trabajo</strong> This is a noun that means "work" in English. Combined with "su", it refers to "her work". The phrase "todo su trabajo" implies all the work she has to do.</div>','<h2>Yo iré a casa cuando haya terminado de buscar flores.</h2><div><strong>Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>iré</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense form of the verb "ir", which means "to go". It indicates a future action of the subject going somewhere.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated as "to" in English. It’s used before "casa" to indicate direction.</div><div><strong>casa</strong> This word means "home" or "house" in Spanish. Here, it’s the destination or the place where the subject will go.</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish meaning "when". It introduces a subordinate clause indicating the time at which the main clause will happen.</div><div><strong>haya terminado</strong> This verb phrase is in the present perfect subjunctive tense (haya is the present subjunctive form of "haber" and "terminado" is the past participle of "terminar"), which means "have finished" in English. It describes an action that needs to be completed in the future before another action can take place.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that can translate to "of" or "from" in English, but in this context, it is part of a verbal phrase that translates to "to" when used with "terminar", indicating the action of finishing.</div><div><strong>buscar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to search for" or "to look for" in Spanish. It specifies what action is being finished.</div><div><strong>flores</strong> This word means "flowers" in Spanish and is the object of the action "buscar". It specifies what the subject is searching for.</div>','<h2>Casi lloré de alegría cuando llegó el avión.</h2><div><strong>Casi</strong> This is the Spanish adverb that translates to "almost". It indicates that the action was very close to occurring, but didn’t fully occur.</div><div><strong>lloré</strong> This is the first-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "llorar", which means "to cry". It shows a completed past action.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This Spanish preposition can be translated as "of" or "from". In this case, it is used to link the verb "lloré" to the reason for crying, "alegría".</div><div><strong>alegría</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "joy" or "happiness".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction meaning "when". It introduces a time clause that specifies when the action of crying occurred.</div><div><strong>llegó</strong> This is the third-person singular preterite tense of the verb "llegar", which means "to arrive". This conveys a completed action of the plane arriving in the past.</div><div><strong>el avión</strong> This is a Spanish noun phrase where "el" is the masculine singular definite article "the" and "avión" means "airplane". Together, it translates to "the airplane".</div>','<h2>Te encantaba ir a la escuela cuando eras joven.</h2><div><strong>Te</strong> This is a reflexive/pronoun in Spanish, which is used here as an indirect object pronoun equivalent to "you" in English. It indicates that the action of the verb is directed towards "you".</div><div><strong>encantaba</strong> This comes from the verb "encantar", which means "to love" or "to delight". Here, it’s in the imperfect tense third-person singular form, indicating an ongoing or repeated past action that can be translated as "you loved" or "was enchanting to you". It’s used in Spanish to express that someone liked something very much.</div><div><strong>ir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to go" in Spanish. It is used after "encantaba" to indicate the action that was loved/took place in the past.</div><div><strong>a la escuela</strong> This phrase means "to school". "A" translates to "to", and "la escuela" is a feminine noun that means "the school".</div><div><strong>cuando</strong> This is an adverb of time in Spanish, meaning "when". It introduces a time clause in the sentence.</div><div><strong>eras</strong> This comes from the verb "ser", which means "to be". Here, it’s in the imperfect tense second-person singular form, translating to "you were". It describes a past state of being.</div><div><strong>joven</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "young". It’s used here in the singular form to agree with the subject "you".</div>','<h2>Me molesta que tú te preocupes tanto.</h2><div><strong>Me</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in indirect object position in Spanish, which could be translated to "me" in English. It indicates that the speaker is affected by the action.</div><div><strong>molesta</strong> This is the third-person singular of the verb "molestar" in the present tense, meaning "to bother" or "to annoy". Here it is used impersonally to mean "it bothers (me)".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish, commonly used to introduce subordinate clauses. In this case, it means "that" as it introduces the clause "you worry so much".</div><div><strong>tú</strong> This is the second-person singular subject pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in the second-person singular, used here as part of the reflexive verb "preocuparse", which means "to worry". It indicates that the action is performed upon the subject itself.</div><div><strong>preocupes</strong> This is the second-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "preocuparse", following the conjunction "que". The subjunctive mood is used because it expresses a reaction to a non-definite or subjective situation or state.</div><div><strong>tanto</strong> This is an adverb meaning "so much" or "too much" in English. It modifies the verb "preocupes", indicating the extent of the worry.</div>','<h2>Te sugiero que tú le traigas flores.</h2><div><strong>Te</strong> This is a second-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "to you" in English. It’s used here to indicate the person to whom the suggestion is made.</div><div><strong>sugiero</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "sugerir", which means "to suggest". I am offering a suggestion to you.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction that often introduces a subordinate clause. In this case, it introduces the suggestion itself.</div><div><strong>tú</strong> This is the second-person singular subject pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It emphasizes the subject performing the action in the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is a third-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "him, her, or it" in English. It is used here to indicate the entity (masculine or feminine) that should receive the flowers.</div><div><strong>traigas</strong> This is the second-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "traer", which means "to bring". The subjunctive mood is often used in subordinate clauses following expressions of wish, suggestion, or command.</div><div><strong>flores</strong> This is a noun in Spanish, meaning "flowers". It is the direct object of the verb "traigas", indicating what you should bring.</div>','<h2>Espero que ellos puedan venir con nosotros.</h2><div><strong>Espero</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "esperar", which means "to hope" or "to expect" in English. It indicates the speaker is conveying their hope or expectation.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word is a conjunction in Spanish, often used to link a main clause with a dependent clause. In this sentence, "que" introduces the dependent clause that expresses what the speaker is hoping for.</div><div><strong>ellos</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the dependent clause, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>puedan</strong> This is the third-person plural present subjunctive form of the verb "poder", which means "to be able to" or "can" in English. The subjunctive mood is used because the sentence expresses a hope or a wish, rather than asserting a fact.</div><div><strong>venir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "venir", which means "to come" in English. It is the main verb in the dependent clause, following "puedan" to form the phrase "puedan venir" (can come).</div><div><strong>con</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish meaning "with". It introduces the phrase indicating the company in which the subject is hoped to come.</div><div><strong>nosotros</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "us" in English. It indicates that the speaker is included in the group that the others are hoped to accompany.</div>','<h2>Soñé que caían libros del cielo.</h2><div><strong>Soñé</strong> This is the first-person singular preterite tense form of the Spanish verb "soñar", which means "to dream". It indicates that the speaker experienced a dream.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction that translates to "that" in English. It introduces a subordinate clause, providing detail about the dream.</div><div><strong>caían</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "caer", which means "to fall". It indicates a continuous or repeated past action, as in "were falling" in English.</div><div><strong>libros</strong> This is the Spanish word for "books". It is a plural noun and serves as the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>del cielo</strong> This Spanish phrase translates to "from the sky" in English. "Del" is a contraction of "de" (of/from) and "el" (the), and "cielo" means "sky". It indicates the origin of where the books were falling from.</div>','<h2>Me asusta que nosotros fumemos demasiado.</h2><div><strong>Me</strong> This is the first-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "to me" or "for me" in English. It’s used here to express that something causes a reaction or feeling to the speaker.</div><div><strong>asusta</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "asustar", which means "to scare" or "to frighten". In this context, it’s used reflexively to mean "it scares me" with "me" as the indirect object.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that most often means "that". It’s used to introduce a noun clause here.</div><div><strong>nosotros</strong> This is the first-person plural subject pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>fumemos</strong> This is the first-person plural present subjunctive tense form of the verb "fumar", which means "to smoke". The subjunctive is used here to express a subjective action or state that is being feared, doubted, or wished upon by the speaker.</div><div><strong>demasiado</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish meaning "too much" or "excessively". It’s modifying the verb "fumemos" to indicate the amount or extent to which the action of smoking is taking place.</div>','<h2>Yo creo que te va a gustar tu regalo.</h2><div><strong>Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>creo</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "creer", which means "to believe" or "to think". It indicates what the subject believes or thinks.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word translates to "that" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a dependent clause.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is the second-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It indicates to whom the action of the verb is directed.</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ir" when used in conjunction with "a", it constructs a periphrastic future tense. It is equivalent to "is going to" in English.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This preposition is used here as part of the periphrastic future tense, combined with "va" (from the verb "ir") to indicate a future action.</div><div><strong>gustar</strong> This verb means "to like" in English. However, unlike English, the thing that is liked is the subject of the sentence in Spanish, and the person who likes it is the indirect object (in this case, "te").</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular possessive adjective in Spanish, equivalent to "your" in English. It shows ownership or relation to the second person.</div><div><strong>regalo</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "gift" or "present". It is the subject in the clause and is what is thought to be liked in this sentence.</div>','<h2>Es la primera vez que ellas juegan golf.</h2><div><strong>Es</strong> This is the third-person singular of the verb "ser" in present tense. It translates to "is" in English and is used here as a copular verb to link the subject to the predicate.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is a definite article in Spanish, used here in the feminine singular form. It translates to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>primera vez</strong> This phrase means "first time" in English. It is a feminine noun phrase where "primera" is an ordinal adjective that agrees in gender and number with "vez".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that is often used to introduce relative clauses. In this case, it can be translated as "that" in English.</div><div><strong>ellas</strong> This is a personal pronoun in the third-person plural feminine form. It translates to "they" in English and refers to a group of females.</div><div><strong>juegan</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "jugar", which means "to play" in English. It describes the action they are performing.</div><div><strong>golf</strong> This is a noun, borrowed from English, that remains the same in Spanish. It refers to the sport of golf.</div>','<h2>Siento que ellos juzguen a otras personas.</h2><div><strong>Siento</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "sentir", which means "to feel" in English. It reflects the speaker’s emotional or sensory reaction.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word translates to "that" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a dependent clause following the main clause "siento".</div><div><strong>ellos</strong> This is the third-person plural subject pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "they" in English. It refers to a group of people that the speaker is discussing.</div><div><strong>juzguen</strong> This is the third-person plural present subjunctive form of the verb "juzgar", which means "to judge" in English. The subjunctive mood is used to express doubts, wishes, conjectures, and possibilities.</div><div><strong>a otras personas</strong> This phrase translates to "other people" in English. "a" is a preposition that in this context is used to indicate the direct object of the sentence, and "otras personas" is the direct object noun phrase, specifying whom they judge.</div>','<h2>Yo creo que vamos a trabajar bien juntos.</h2><div><strong>Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>creo</strong> This comes from the Spanish verb "creer", which means "to believe" or "to think". Here, it is conjugated in the first-person singular present tense, expressing a belief or opinion by the speaker.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "that". It is used to introduce a subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>vamos</strong> Derived from the verb "ir", which means "to go". "Vamos" is the first-person plural present indicative form, meaning "we go", or in this context, it is used to express a future action as "we are going to".</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish commonly used with verbs of movement. In this context, "a" is part of a periphrasis that forms a future tense, as in "going to [do something]". </div><div><strong>trabajar</strong> This is an infinitive verb in Spanish meaning "to work". In this sentence, it forms part of the future tense construction in combination with "vamos a".</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish meaning "well". It modifies the verb "trabajar", indicating the manner in which the work is to be performed.</div><div><strong>juntos</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish meaning "together". It implies collaboration or teamwork between the subjects.</div>','<h2>No hables con personas que no conoces.</h2><div><strong>No</strong> This is the Spanish word for "don’t". It is used here as a negative command or imperative, telling someone not to do something.</div><div><strong>hables</strong> This is the second-person singular present subjunctive form of the verb "hablar", which means "to speak". In this sentence, it is paired with "no" to instruct someone not to engage in the action of speaking.</div><div><strong>con</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that means "with". It is used to indicate association or accompaniment.</div><div><strong>personas</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "people" or "persons". It is the object of the preposition "con" in this sentence.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a relative pronoun in Spanish, meaning "that" or "who". It introduces a subordinate clause, "que no conoces".</div><div><strong>no</strong> In this second part, it is again the Spanish word for "don’t" or "not". It negates the verb in the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>conoces</strong> This is the second-person singular present indicative form of the verb "conocer", which means "to know" or "be familiar with". It completes the subordinate clause, referring to people whom one does not know.</div>','<h2>Es bueno que él haga ejercicios a menudo.</h2><div><strong>Es</strong> This is the third-person singular of the verb "ser" in the present indicative. It means "is" in English and serves as the main verb in the sentence.</div><div><strong>bueno</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "good". It conveys that something is positive or beneficial.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish which means "that". It is used to link the first part of the sentence (it’s good) with the subordinate clause (he does exercises).</div><div><strong>él</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>haga</strong> This is the third-person singular of the verb "hacer" in the present subjunctive. It means "does" or "to do" in English and is used to talk about actions that are desired, hypothetical, or uncertain.</div><div><strong>ejercicios</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "exercises". It refers to the physical activities he’s supposed to perform regularly.</div><div><strong>a menudo</strong> This is a Spanish adverbial phrase meaning "often" or "frequently". It describes the frequency with which the exercises are recommended to be done.</div>','<h2>Quiero que ellos resuelvan sus problemas.</h2><div><strong>Quiero</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the Spanish verb "querer", which means "to want" or "to wish". Here, it expresses the speaker’s desire.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that is commonly used to connect a main clause with a subordinate clause, usually introducing a desire, a demand, or a dependent statement. It can be translated to "that" in English.</div><div><strong>ellos</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "they" in English. It is the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>resuelvan</strong> This is the third-person plural present subjunctive tense form of the Spanish verb "resolver", which means "to resolve" or "to solve". The subjunctive is used because it follows the conjunction "que" and expresses a wish or desire within the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>sus</strong> This is the third-person plural possessive adjective in Spanish. It means "their" in English and shows ownership or association with "ellos", referring to "problemas" (problems).</div><div><strong>problemas</strong> This is the plural form of the Spanish noun "problema", which means "problem". This noun is the direct object of the verb "resuelvan", indicating what the speaker wants "ellos" to resolve.</div>','<h2>Ojalá que yo empiece a entenderlo pronto.</h2><div><strong>Ojalá</strong> This word expresses a hope or wish for something to happen. It doesn’t have a direct English translation but is similar to "hopefully" or "I hope that". Ojalá is originally from Arabic, reflecting Spain’s historical connections with the Arab world.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish, and in this context, it connects the word "ojalá" with the following clause. It is equivalent to "that" in English when used in this way.</div><div><strong>yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>empiece</strong> This is the first-person singular present subjunctive tense of the verb "empezar", which means "to begin" or "to start". The subjunctive mood is used to express wishes, hopes, and uncertainty.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This preposition is often used in Spanish to indicate the start of an infinitive phrase, among other uses. In this case, it is part of the phrase "a entenderlo".</div><div><strong>entenderlo</strong> This is the infinitive verb "entender", which means "to understand", combined with the direct object pronoun "lo", which stands for "it". Together, they mean "to understand it".</div><div><strong>pronto</strong> This is an adverb meaning "soon" in English. It indicates the hope is for the understanding to begin in the near future.</div>','<h2>Él les suplica que ustedes firmen el documento.</h2><div><strong>Él</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "he" in English. It is used as the subject of the sentence to refer to a male individual.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in Spanish that can mean "to them" or "to you all" in English when referring to ustedes (formal "you" plural). It indicates to whom the action of the verb is directed.</div><div><strong>suplica</strong> This comes from the Spanish verb "suplicar", meaning "to beg" or "to plead". In this sentence, it is in the third-person singular present indicative form, so it means "he begs" or "he pleads".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "that". It is often used to introduce a subordinate clause, as it does here.</div><div><strong>ustedes</strong> This is the second-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you all" or "you guys" in English. It is used in formal contexts in some Spanish-speaking countries or universally in others.</div><div><strong>firmen</strong> This is a verb in the subjunctive mood, from "firmar", meaning "to sign". It is in the second-person plural form, thus "you (all) sign". In this sentence, the subjunctive is used because the action is something desired or hoped for, not yet a reality.</div><div><strong>el documento</strong> This is a Spanish noun phrase meaning "the document" in English. "El" is the definite article "the", and "documento" means "document".</div>','<h2>Yo creo que ellas van a odiar la película.</h2><div><strong>Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>creo</strong> This comes from the verb "creer", which means "to believe" or "to think". Here it is conjugated in the first-person singular present tense, meaning "I think" or "I believe".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish, which translates to "that" in English. It is used to introduce a subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>ellas</strong> This is the third-person plural feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "they" when referring to a group of females in English.</div><div><strong>van</strong> This comes from the verb "ir", which means "to go". Here it is conjugated in the third-person plural present tense, so it means "they go". In this sentence, it is used with "a" to form the future tense construction, equivalent to "they are going to".</div><div><strong>a</strong> This preposition is used in Spanish to form the periphrastic future tense, equivalent to "going to" in English.</div><div><strong>odiar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to hate" in Spanish. Here it is used after "van a", forming the structure for the future tense: "van a odiar" ("they are going to hate").</div><div><strong>la película</strong> This phrase is formed by the definite article "la" meaning "the" in English, and the noun "película", which means "movie" or "film". Together, "la película" means "the movie" or "the film".</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);